<!--
  Plan-only probe for the narrow-entrance recovery: ask the global planner
  whether a route to {goal} exists, and never command the base.

  Why this exists (2026-11-21, red_cube_1 -> C): the recovery used to infer
  "the planner refused" from a NavigateToPose that failed within a few seconds.
  That inference is unsound twice over. A plain goal is NOT side-effect free -
  both nav_to_pose_short_recovery.xml and nav_to_pose_yield_pause.xml wrap the
  pipeline in a RecoveryNode that clears BOTH costmaps, then backs up 0.5 m,
  then spins 0.8 rad - so "a fast failure" can leave the base somewhere else
  entirely, and the next "planning refusal" is then judged from a pose the base
  was driven to by Nav2's own recovery. And the same fast failure can come from
  a behaviour tree that never loaded.

  This tree contains exactly one action: ComputePathToPose. Success means the
  global planner produced a route from the CURRENT start cell; failure means it
  did not. Nothing else runs, so the answer is a property of the map and the
  footprint at that instant, and no motion, clearing or waiting is implied.

  Do not add a recovery node here: the whole point is that a probe failure says
  "no route exists right now", not "something was tried".
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
